---
<p><code>main</code> can take command-line arguments as a parameter. There is no flag parser yet, so flags and subcommands are a loop you write, with errors as values like any others.</p>

<h2 id="args">Read the positional arguments</h2>
<p><strong>Runnable.</strong> <code>void main(ReadOnlySpan&lt;string&gt; args)</code> gets the arguments after the program name, as UTF-8 strings. Pass them to <code>bayan run</code> after <code>--</code>.</p>
<example name="cookbook-cli-args"/>
<pre><code class="other" data-lang="shell">$ bayan run cookbook-cli-args.byn -- salam 42
2 arguments
0: salam
1: 42</code></pre>
<pre><code class="other" data-lang="Python">args = sys.argv[1:]</code></pre>
<note>The spec lists arguments as a source of <code>Untrusted&lt;T&gt;</code> (§15.1); today <code>main</code> receives plain <code>string</code>. Treat them as outside input, especially before <code>Io.run</code> or a path built from them.</note>

<h2 id="flags">Parse flags and options</h2>
<p><strong>Runnable by hand; the library is Planned.</strong> Loop over <code>args</code>, compare, and convert with <code>i32.tryParse</code>, which returns <code>null</code> instead of throwing for non-numbers; <code>i64.parse</code> gives a <code>ParseError</code> naming the bad byte. The spec lists <code>Args</code> among the standard derivations (§5.3), but derivations are "not in v0.1". The example parses a fixed list when you pass no arguments, so its output never changes.</p>
<example name="cookbook-cli-flags"/>
<pre><code class="other" data-lang="Go">verbose := flag.Bool("v", false, "verbose")
count := flag.Int("count", 1, "how many")
flag.Parse()</code></pre>

<h2 id="subcommands">Parse subcommands</h2>
<p><strong>Runnable by hand.</strong> The first argument is the subcommand. Declare its failure modes as a <code>union error</code>, return it with <code>fail</code>, and turn it into a message with <code>catch switch</code>. Nothing is thrown, and the compiler checks that every case is covered.</p>
<example name="cookbook-cli-subcommands"/>
<p>A failing <code>main</code> exits nonzero; the spec lets each error case choose a code with <code>@exitCode(n)</code> (§8), which this page did not run.</p>

<h2 id="env">Read environment variables</h2>
<p><strong>Not in the standard library yet.</strong> The compiler defines no <code>Env</code>. The spec reserves <code>env</code> for "Environment variables and process-wide settings" (§14): a reader will declare <code>uses env</code>, but no read call is defined.</p>
